	doi = {10.1038/s41586-019-1479-6},
	abstract = {Machines made of soft materials bridge life sciences and engineering1. Advances in soft materials have led to skin-like sensors and muscle-like actuators for soft robots and wearable devices1{{\textendash}}3. Flexible or stretchable counterparts of most key mechatronic components have been developed4,5, principally using fluidically driven systems6{{\textendash}}8; other reported mechanisms include electrostatic9{{\textendash}}12, stimuli-responsive gels13,14 and thermally responsive materials such as liquid metals15{{\textendash}}17 and shape-memory polymers18. Despite the widespread use of fluidic actuation, there have been few soft counterparts of pumps or compressors, limiting the portability and autonomy of soft machines4,8. Here we describe a class of soft-matter bidirectional pumps based on charge-injection electrohydrodynamics19. These solid-state pumps are flexible, stretchable, modular, scalable, quiet and rapid. By integrating the pump into a glove, we demonstrate wearable active thermal management. Embedding the pump in an inflatable structure produces a self-contained fluidic {{\textquoteleft}}muscle{{\textquoteright}}. The stretchable pumps have potential uses in wearable laboratory-on-a-chip and microfluidic sensors, thermally active clothing and autonomous soft robots.},
